      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\) given that \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\), \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circle, and \\(O\\) is the center of the circle. Here's the step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Identify Key Angles and Relationships:**\n   - Since \\(AB\\) is a diameter, \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\) (angle in a semicircle).\n   - The tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are perpendicular to the radii at those points, so \\(\\angle OBE = \\angle OEB = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - The tangent at \\(B\\) and the tangent at \\(E\\) intersect at point \\(C\\) at an angle that is supplementary to \\(\\angle BAE\\) because the tangents form a linear pair with the angle subtended by the diameter.\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle BEC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAE = 180^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 137^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Determine \\(\\angle CED\\):**\n   - Since \\(O\\) is the center of the circle, \\(OE\\) is a radius and \\(BE\\) is a tangent to the circle at \\(E\\).\n   - The angle \\(\\angle OEB\\) is a right angle, and since \\(OE\\) is a radius, \\(\\angle OEB = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle OEC = 180^\\circ - \\angle OEB - \\angle BEC = 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ - 137^\\circ = -47^\\circ\\) (but we take the positive equivalent, i.e., \\(180^\\circ - 47^\\circ = 133^\\circ\\)).\n\n3. **Use the property of cyclic quadrilateral \\(CEBD\\):**\n   - Since \\(CEBD\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral, the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle CED = 180^\\circ - \\angle BEC = 180^\\circ - 137^\\circ = 43^\\circ\\).\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\) is \\(\\boxed{43}\\).",
